obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={e}^{τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221158 1074 "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={e}^{τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 false] [17592186221163 1074 "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221163 1074 "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} & =L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 false] [17592186221430 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442018 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221430 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442020 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442022 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442019 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442021 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221464 1050 780.0 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221938 1071 "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221938 1071 "Given that $L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$ for $s > 0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}τ^{2}dτ$\nfor $s > 0$." 13194139534871 17592186045417 false] [17592186221938 1074 "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={t}^{2}.$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221940 1071 "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186221940 1071 "For the function\n$G(s) = \\frac{2}{s^{4}}, s > 0$\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s)$, given that\n$L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$." 13194139534871 17592186045417 false] [17592186221940 1074 "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}}\\} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186222478 1064 "Suppose a quantity increases over time at a rate proportional to its current value, such as the balance of a bank account growing with continuous interest. The Laplace transform can be used to analyze how such exponential growth behaves, especially when the function is **integrated over time** to represent total accumulation.\n\nTo illustrate this, we’ll find the Laplace transform of a function defined as the integral of an exponential function. This shows how *integration in the time domain* corresponds to *division by $s$* in the $s$-domain.\n\nSuppose we are given that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s>a.\n$$\n\nLet's find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\int_{0}^{t} e^{\\tau/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau,\n\\qquad s > \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe apply the **Integration in Time Property**: if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=e^{t/2}$. Thus, for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{t/2}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-\\frac{1}{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\,\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ/2}\\,dτ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s\\,(s-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s(2s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 true] [17592186222478 1064 "Suppose a quantity increases over time at a rate proportional to its current value, such as the balance of a bank account growing with continuous interest. The Laplace transform can be used to analyze how such exponential growth behaves, especially when the function is **integrated over time** to represent total accumulation.\n\nTo illustrate this, we’ll find the Laplace transform of a function defined as the integral of an exponential function. This shows how *integration in the time domain* corresponds to *division by $s$* in the $s$-domain.\n\nSuppose we are given that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s>a.\n$$\n\nLet's find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\int_{0}^{t} e^{\\tau/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau,\n\\qquad s > \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe apply the **Integration in Time Property**: if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=e^{t/2}$. Thus, for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{t/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-\\frac{1}{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L\\,{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ/2}\\,dτ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s\\,(s-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s(2s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 17592186045417 false] [17592186302374 1071 "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{\\o